5:[["$","$L10","5.2",{"topic":{"id":"5.2","title":"Threat Intelligence Lifecycle","minutes":13,"summary":"Make intelligence repeatable through direction, collection, processing, analysis, dissemination, and feedback.","sections":[{"title":"Direction and collection","body":["Define priority requirements, scope, assumptions, and deadlines. Identify gaps in current knowledge and choose sources that can answer the question. Sources may include internal telemetry, incident reports, public advisories, provider notices, and carefully governed sharing communities. A collection plan should record authority, handling restrictions, and expected value.","Avoid collecting everything because storage is cheap. Excess data increases privacy risk and analyst workload. For each source, record origin, access method, time coverage, reliability, and limitations. An internal authentication log and a public actor report answer different questions and should not be treated as interchangeable."]},{"title":"Processing and analysis","body":["Normalize formats, timestamps, identifiers, and confidence labels without losing the original record. Deduplicate indicators and preserve relationships. Translate raw observations into a form suitable for analysis while recording transformations. A normalized domain should not accidentally become a claim that the domain is malicious.","Evaluate hypotheses about campaigns, targeting, and behavior. Compare new observations with existing knowledge and look for disconfirming evidence. The Diamond Model can organize adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim relationships; ATT&CK can describe techniques. Neither model removes the need for source evaluation or causal reasoning."]},{"title":"Dissemination and feedback","body":["Deliver the right product to the right audience in a usable format and within the decision window. Include key judgments, evidence, confidence, recommended actions, and expiry or review dates. Separate intelligence from operational instructions that require additional authorization. A suggested containment action is not itself permission to execute it.","Ask whether the product changed a decision, improved detection, or reduced uncertainty. Track false positives, missed threats, and stale indicators. Feed those results into new requirements and source evaluation. A lifecycle without feedback becomes a publication pipeline rather than a learning process."]}],"practice":{"title":"Run a miniature intelligence cycle","scenario":"A suspicious login pattern appears in a fictional university dataset.","steps":["Define one priority question and collect the relevant synthetic records.","Normalize timestamps and evaluate the source.","Compare credential theft with a legitimate travel or configuration change.","Deliver a short assessment and define one feedback measure."],"debrief":"The cycle is iterative. Feedback can reveal that the initial requirement was too broad or that a source was less reliable than assumed."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","dpdp"],"sources":["first","mitre","nist-csf"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain direction and collection and apply it to an investigation.","Explain processing and analysis and apply it to an investigation.","Explain dissemination and feedback and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.2","prompt":"What is the purpose of the feedback stage?","options":["To delete all earlier assessments.","To improve requirements, sources, and products using observed outcomes.","To guarantee that every alert is correct.","To replace analysis with automated sharing."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Feedback turns intelligence into a learning process and helps measure whether it supported better decisions.","unit":5}}}]]
